<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhsin ibn Fadl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1827">1827</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhsin ibn Fadl al-Abdali was sultan of Lahej in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> from 1827 to 1847. In January 1839 a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> force under Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haine">Haines</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> from him after disputes over a plundered ship, creating the Aden Settlement. Muhsin later accepted a British stipend, and Lahej became the leading protected state in Aden's hinterland.</p></div>
